ef 17592186267764 13194139534808 17592186045417 false] [17592186268639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436699 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436706 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436714 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436715 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436719 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268639 1078 #edb/ref 17592186267485 13194139534808 17592186045417 false] [17592186268639 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436719 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268899 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268899 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 false] [17592186268902 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268902 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 false] [17592186268904 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 true] [17592186268904 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534808 17592186045417 false] [17592186335822 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the